# changelogger/CHANGELOG

## 20261031

0.24.0: + plan, which describes each revision in revisions.yml for git-import-all, and + build, which writes the CHANGELOGs from it.

1. + plan: writes revisions.yml into the revisions root, each revision's number, day and commit message, from the entries which wrote the CHANGELOGs before; an existing one is not replaced, being the record once written. A revision with no date or no message is written with `????-??-??` or an empty message and named on stderr; entries from a CHANGELOG which do not number the revisions write nothing. + planned_entries(), + note_gaps(), + revisions(), + revisions_yaml(), + day(), + revisions_path().
2. + build: writes every revision's CHANGELOG from revisions.yml, into the one it holds under whatever name, where it is not already what would be written; it writes nothing from a file git-import-all would refuse, checked as git-import-all 0.17.0 checks it. + -n, --dry-run. + recorded_entries(), + recorded_list(), + recorded_faults(), + entry_faults(), + day_of(), + changelog_report(); /write_changelog()/changelog_content()/, + write_changelog().
3. ~ main(): plan or build, one being required. + command(), + validate_command(), + take_option(), + dry_run?(), + usage_error(); ~ parse_arguments(): -f, --header-file is plan's.
4. + Changelog: taken from git-import-all 0.16.0, its CHANGELOG reader, for a date in each heading, several versions beneath one and entries stating none among them, and for a version in each heading. ~ Changelog::Format::Common#entry(): + body:, the entry's whole text. ~ Changelog::Format::DatedHeading: + `## ????????`; a block keeps its lines' indentation. ~ Changelog::Format::VersionedHeading#parse(): the body's first line is the heading's version and summary, the summary where the heading has none being a line of prose standing alone beneath it. + Changelog::NotUnderstood.
5. ~ entries_from(): reads a revision's CHANGELOG with Changelog, whatever its name, rather than with a pattern which read only changelogger's own form; one not understood gives no entries, and says so. + changelog_path(), + changelog_date(); ~ changelog_exist?().
6. ~ heading_date(): no longer says ???????? is written, plan writing `????-??-??` as the time.
7. + require 'yaml'.
8. ~ test/changelogger_test.rb: + changelogger(), ~ run_changelogger(): plan and then build. + seventeen examples: plan's file, its shape and order, messages with items, the bare day, no CHANGELOG written, an existing file kept, a CHANGELOG in either form and one not numbering the revisions, a missing date planned and then given; build from the file as it stands, nothing rewritten where nothing changed, a full time's day, an entry out of place refused, no file, a dry run; a subcommand wanted, and -f being plan's. ~ four examples: a CHANGELOG not understood left alone, two read when planning again, and ????-??-?? planned.
9. ~ README.md: + plan and build; + revisions.yml; /Output/The CHANGELOGs/.
10. ~ TODO: ~ item 1, for plan; ~ item 6, for the reader taken from git-import-all.
11. ~ bin/changelogger: + plan, build and revisions.yml in the header's description and examples; /0.23.0/0.24.0/

## 20261011

0.23.0: Take no section heading for the program's name where a header has no name line.

The name is the first comment line holding a single word, which in a header carrying one is the program's own, `# redback`. A header without one gave up the first heading which stood alone instead: Redback's 0.2 headers open with `# Date:` and then `# Changes: `, so their name was read as `Changes:`, and 0.3.0, the first revision to carry `# redback`, was written with a change of name, `1. /Changes:/redback/`, which went into its commit subject and into the 127 CHANGELOGs after it.

1. ~ parse_name(): a word ending in a colon is passed over, being a section's heading rather than a name.
2. Over Redback's 272 revisions, only 0.3.0's entry changes, and no CHANGELOG holds `/Changes:/`. Over SimpleCSV's 84, no entry changes.
3. ~ test/changelogger_test.rb: + one example, run to 50, failing against 0.22.0.
4. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.22.0/0.23.0/

0.22.0: Set aside a date which is no date at all, such as 20060631, and take the modification time in its place.

A header's date was taken as written, real or not. Redback's 0.6.1 states `# Date: 20060631`, June's thirty-first, which changelogger wrote as its heading; git-import-all could not parse it, set no author date, and the commit took the day of the import. The file was made and last saved on 20060831 at 14:57, its header having been copied from 0.6.0's `20060629` with the day changed and the month not.

1. + real_date?(): whether a date as written is a day which was, by Date.valid_date?.
2. ~ heading_date(): a date which is not one is set aside, so that the modification time is taken as it is where none is stated, and the warning says which it was: `251: 20060631 in the script header is not a date; took 20060831 from the modification time of lib/Redback.rb`. Over Redback's 272 revisions, 0.6.1 is the one, and it comes out 20060831.
3. + require 'date'.
4. ~ test/changelogger_test.rb: + two examples, run to 49. Against 0.21.1 the one setting June's thirty-first aside fails; the one keeping a leap day passes either way, being a guard.
5. ~ bin/changelogger: + an impossible date in the header's description of the fallback; /0.21.1/0.22.0/

0.21.1: A section's heading ends the Changes section above it when it holds a hyphen, as `# Ad-hoc ideas:` does.

1. + SECTION_HEADING, ~ parse_changes(): a heading was letters and spaces before its colon, so `# Ad-hoc ideas:` did not end the Changes section above it. The heading was written as a `?` item and every idea beneath it as a change: Redback's 0.4.8 gained `5. Roll-in the visited stuff to passes_muster` among what it did. 78 of Redback's revisions carry that heading after their Changes, and 63 of their entries were so swollen; its warnings for what was not understood fell from 91 to 16, those left being its own `#5.` and `# 40` written without the space or the stop.
2. Over SimpleCSV's 84 revisions, no entry changes.
3. ~ test/changelogger_test.rb: + one example, run to 47, failing against 0.21.0.
4. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.21.0/0.21.1/

0.21.0: Read a date labelled `# Date: 20051014`, the oldest form a header's date was written in.

1. ~ DATE_LINE: + an optional `Date:` before the date. Such a line was passed over, so 0.20.0 took the header file's modification time in its place, which is near the date the header states but is not it: a file saved again after midnight, or days later, took that day instead. Redback's revisions 26 to 251 write their dates so, 226 of them, and 62 came out a day or more from what their headers say.
2. Over Redback's 272 revisions, only the 26 with no header now fall back upon a modification time.
3. ~ test/changelogger_test.rb: + two examples, run to 46. Against 0.20.0 the one reading the labelled date fails; the one reading the version below it passes either way, being a guard.
