0.19.0: + a tier for a snapshot, a working copy taken between versions, which states no version and is summarised by the whole of its diff.

A revision whose header stated no new version and no new Changes item was written as a version all the same, so the version it carried was stated two, three, or five times over, and git-import-all titled every one of those commits as that version. Summarised by the first line of its diff, a revision in which a fixture was regenerated under the next name and the test script changed read as `- test/6.0.csv`. And a revision identical to the one before it had an entry with nothing in it, which git-import-all could not count, so a tree holding one parsed to fewer entries than it had revisions and the import refused it. Such a revision is a snapshot, not a version, and is now written as one: `~ test/test.rb, test/6.0.csv --> test/6.1.csv`, with no version, a commit titled with a version being a version (decided 20260909).

1. + snapshot?(): a revision identical to the one before it, whatever came before; or one after a version has been stated whose header states no version or the version last stated, and no new Changes item. Before any version is stated there is nothing for a revision to be between, so a history kept without versions is diffed as it always was: Redback's first 145 revisions state no version, and the first 26 of those no date either.
2. + process_snapshot(), + snapshot_summary(): the entry states no version and is summarised by every file changed, `~` for those altered or renamed, `+` for those come and `-` for those gone, three named and the rest counted, as holdings_summary() does. The items are the header's structural diff, where it has one, and then every file, the header file included. Identical, it reads `Unchanged from the revision before.`
3. + renames(): a file gone and one come, alone of their kind (directory and extension) in one directory, are taken for one renamed and written `test/6.0.csv --> test/6.1.csv`. Paired by name rather than by content, SimpleCSV's regenerated fixtures sharing no line with those they replaced; where two of a kind went, nothing is paired.
4. + revision_files(), + identical?(), + file_changes(), + file_items(), + version_of(), + version_before(), + structural_diff_of().
5. revision_files() passes over the CHANGELOG itself and not whatever begins so. non_binary_files() passes over any file whose name begins with changelog, changelogger.rb among them, which this tier met at once: the suite's revisions root is named changelogger..., and a library named for it was taken for a CHANGELOG. That is left as it was here, being another change.
6. Over SimpleCSV's 84 revisions, exactly the seven TODO item 5 named became snapshots: 21 (`0.2.2`), 38 to 41 (`0.4.6`), 45 (`0.4.9`), and 72 (the second `0.9.3`); every other entry is as 0.18.2 wrote it. git-import-all 0.16.0 parsed 84 entries and wrote 84 commits.
7. ~ test/changelogger_test.rb: + five examples, run to 40. Against 0.18.2 the three which assert a snapshot fail; the two which assert a history without versions and a new Changes item under the same version pass either way, being guards.
8. ~ README.md: + the fourth tier; /a complete snapshot of the project/a complete copy of the project/, snapshot now naming the tier.
9. ~ TODO: - 5, done; /6/5/, /7/6/.
10. ~ bin/changelogger: + tier 4 in the header's description; /0.18.2/0.19.0/

0.18.2: Read a version of more than three parts.

1. ~ parse_version(): a version of four parts, `0.6.0.0`, was read as none, the pattern wanting exactly three. Its entry stated no version, and with no summary of its own its subject line was left empty, so the entry began with a blank line which its date heading then stood above alone. SimpleCSV's revision 59 is the one so stated among its 84, and Files' revision 15 is another; git-import-all took 59's first item for its subject. Two parts are not read as a version, a bare comment of two numbers being as likely something else.
2. Wanted before the snapshot tier, which takes a revision stating no new version, after a version has been stated, for a snapshot: 59, being read as stating none, was taken for one.
3. ~ test/changelogger_test.rb: + one example, run to 35, failing against 0.18.1.
4. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.18.1/0.18.2/


