## 20260824

0.16.0: An item names the file it was read from, as an entry written by hand does.

1. + bin/changelogger: + within(): prefixes each structural item with the file it came from, relative to the revision directory, so that tier 2 writes `~ bin/tap-audit: + usage()` where it wrote `+ usage()` and tier 3 does likewise. The path is the one find_binary already settled upon, so a library's items name lib/Browser.rb and a program's name bin/<program>.
2. ~ bin/changelogger: ~ process_initial_version(), ~ process_subsequent_version(): the two places a structural item is made.
3. Tier 1's items are not passed through it, being written by hand and saying what they mean already; nor is a name change, which names the program rather than anything within it.
4. Why: every entry written by hand in these repositories names its file, and tier 2's did not, so an entry taken from the generator had to have it added afterwards. tap-audit's fifteen revisions were prefixed by hand across 130 items after the fact, which is the work this removes.
5. ~ test/changelogger_test.rb: + five examples, run to 24. Against 0.15.0 the three which assert a prefix fail; the two which assert its absence pass either way, there being no prefix to find, and are guards rather than regressions.
6. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.15.0/0.16.0/

0.15.0: A skipped revision contributes what it holds, having contributed nothing.

1. ~ bin/changelogger: ~ main(): a skipped revision set previous_directory and went on, so its entry never reached the accumulator the next CHANGELOG is built from. Every revision after a skipped one was short by an entry apiece, and the last by as many as had been skipped. Skipping is the normal state, being what protects an entry written by hand, so this was the usual case rather than an edge of it.
2. + bin/changelogger: + entries_from(): the entries a CHANGELOG already holds, read back from the file. A revision is skipped precisely because what it says has been written by hand, so regenerating its entry for the accumulator would push the generated text into every revision after it. What it holds is cumulative, so it stands as the whole accumulator rather than being appended to one.
3. ~ test/changelogger_test.rb: + two examples, run to 19. Against 0.14.0 both fail: one counts the entries three revisions carry with the first skipped, the other reads a hand-written summary out of a later revision's file.
4. Met upon tap-audit's fifteen revisions, where revision 14's CHANGELOG came out holding its own entry alone and had to be rebuilt by hand.
5. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.14.0/0.15.0/, and the date.


## 20260823

0.14.0: + a date line carrying more than one date, the last being the completion date.

1. + expand_dates(): a header's date line may carry several dates, the work having spanned days, and only as much as changes is written. An element escalates against the one before it: one or two digits a day within the same month, four MMDD within the same year, eight a date outright, and a line may escalate mid-way — `20091218, 19, 20100112`. The last is the completion date, which is the one a version takes.
2. ~ parse_date(): `/\A# (\d{8})\s*\z/` took a lone date and nothing else, so every multi-date header wrote ???????? into its heading and the date had to be supplied by hand afterwards. A retired form, but the headers carrying it are not going to be rewritten: it is the record of when the work happened.
3. Run against the Browser library's sixteen revisions, three headings move from ???????? to a date: 20221205, 20230102 and 20250925, the last from `20250922, 23, 24, 25`. git-import-all parses fifteen entries where it parsed fourteen, an entry having been merged into its neighbour for want of a heading to separate them.
4. + two examples, run to 17, both failing against 0.13.0.
5. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.13.0/0.14.0/

0.13.0: + a library's header, read from lib/ where there is no bin/, and --header-file naming one outright.

1. + find_library(): a library keeps its header in lib/, named for the project, where a program carries one alone in bin/. Preferred is the file named for the revisions root, matched without regard to case; failing that the only .rb there, two being nothing to choose between. Until now changelogger read bin/ or nothing, so every library fell to tier 2 and was written out as `## ????????` with no version, no summary and a bare list of files changed.
2. + -f, --header-file: the header named outright, relative to each numbered directory. It settles which file is read, which is the only way a project holding both bin/ and lib/ can say which it means, and it covers a library whose name is not the revisions root's.
3. ~ find_binary(): the switch first, then bin/, then lib/. bin/ before lib/ keeps every project which already worked reading exactly what it read before.
4. No new dependency: the switch is read from ARGV rather than through switches.rb, this program having carried no require at all and its formula no resource.
5. + four examples, run to 15. Against 0.12.1 the two library ones fail and the other two pass, so the first two catch this and the second two guard what was already right — the bin/ preference, and the section header which 0.12.1 fixed and nothing had covered.
6. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.12.1/0.13.0/

0.12.1: A section header ends the Changes section whether or not text follows its colon.

1. ~ parse_changes(): a section header stopped the Changes section only when nothing followed its colon, the test being `/\A# [A-Z][A-Za-z ]*:\s*\z/`. `# Todo:` matched and stopped it. `# History: I realised when wanting to dump all bookmarks...` did not, so that paragraph and everything below it was read as part of the Changes section and written out as unparsed `?` items, four of them in every entry the section reached. Anchoring upon the colon alone stops both.
2. Met upon the Browser library, whose lib/Browser.rb carries Changes, then History, then Todo. changelogger cannot read a library's header at all — it looks in bin/ — so this was reached by putting that header where bin/ is looked for, which is what 0.13.0 is for.
3. Nothing which already worked moves: run before and after against dump_browser_history_to_csv and open_browser_history_from_csv, sixteen revisions between them, the CHANGELOG written is identical either way. The eleven examples pass.
4. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.12.0/0.12.1/

0.12.0: + test/, MiniTest spec style, running against revisions built for each example.

1. + test/changelogger_test.rb: eleven examples, each building its own numbered revisions in a temporary directory and reading the CHANGELOG which was written into them. The revisions root is the whole of the interface and was already there, so nothing needed adding to the program to make it testable. Modelled upon audit_requires' test, which builds a gem home per example for the same reason: what is installed upon this machine, or staged beside it, decides nothing.
2. + CHANGELOGGER, an environment variable naming the binary under test, so that the suite can be pointed at another revision. Run against 0.10.1 two of the eleven fail — the two written for the fault 0.11.0 fixed — which is how they are known to catch it rather than merely to pass here.
3. The other nine pass against 0.10.1, pinning behaviour which was already right: tier 1 taking a Changes section as written, tier 3 inventorying the first revision, methods added and removed and altered, the cumulative order, the skip which leaves a hand-written entry alone, and the name change tier 2 cannot see from structure. Guards rather than regressions, and worth saying which is which.
4. No Rakefile, the tap's other programs having none and this being run as `ruby test/changelogger_test.rb`. moby carries one because moby is a gem.
5. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.11.0/0.12.0/

0.11.0: Read a method's end as its own, a modifier having been counted as opening a block.

1. ~ extract_method_body(): the depth count took any line holding `if`, `unless`, `while`, `until`, `do`, `def`, `class`, `module`, `case`, `for` or `begin` as opening a block. A modifier opens nothing and carries no end — `return nil if x` and `depth = n if line =~ //` are ordinary Ruby and appear throughout — so the count never came back down and the extraction ran past the method's own end into the rest of the file. tap-audit's install_body is sixteen lines and was read as six hundred and eighty, ending at `main`.
2. + BLOCK_OPENERS, + BLOCK_DO, + opens_block?(), + closes_block?(): a keyword opens a block where it begins the expression, which is what the line-start anchor says, and `do` where it ends the line, with or without its parameters. A `do` elsewhere upon the line belongs to a one-line block, which closes itself.
3. What this was doing to the record: every method holding a modifier was reported as altered whenever anything below it changed, tier 2's `~` items being the only thing which reads a body. Run across tap-audit's thirteen revisions before and after, the count went from 802 to 181, so 621 were false. The `+` and `-` items are unaffected and were identical across all thirteen either way, those being read from the sets of names rather than from any body.
4. It is the tier which needs the most editing afterwards which was the least trustworthy. Anything generated by tier 2 and edited into a repository carries this: gem-homepage's CHANGELOG was written that way, and moby's backfill below 0.7.0 may have been.
5. ~ bin/changelogger: /0.10.1/0.11.0/, and the date.


